  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 AM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost and Freeze headlines remain in effect \nthrough early this morning for portions of eastern Virginia and \nnortheast North Carolina. Chilly weather continues through \nTuesday, with additional frost or freeze headlines possible \ntonight where the growing season remains active.\n\nEarly morning weather analysis shows an upper-level low still \nin place from Atlantic Canada into coastal New England, with a \nstrong attending trough extending south into the eastern Gulf \nCoast. At the surface, 1028+ mb high pressure centered over the \nmid-Mississippi River Valley continues to slowly build east \nbehind a surface trough axis oriented well offshore.\n\nTemperatures as of this writing are mainly in the low to mid \n30s just inland from the Chesapeake Bay west to the VA piedmont,\nwith mid 30s to low 40s along the immediate coast, including \nthe Lower Eastern Shore. While skies have cleared out this \nmorning, the boundary layer has remained mixed just enough to \nhold up temperatures along the coast. However, still expect \nwinds to relax a bit just inland, where a Freeze Warning remains\nin place from Bertie County, NC up to the City of Suffolk. A \nFrost Advisory also remains in effect just to the east, across \ninterior portions of eastern Virginia and coastal northeast \nNorth Carolina, where the growing season has not yet \ntechnically ended. The growing season will end across most of \nour counties today, leaving only northeast North Carolina \ncounties east of the Chowan River along with Chesapeake, \nNorfolk, Portsmouth, and Virginia Beach active until later this \nmonth. An updated Public Information Statement will be issued \nlater this morning.\n\nVery little change in sensible weather through tomorrow, as the\nsurface high slowly slides east and the upper-level low \ngradually weakens. After a sunny start, clouds will once again \nincrease late this morning and this afternoon. Nudged forecast \nhighs down slightly, as guidance has been slightly too warm \ngiven the persistent stratocumulus deck over the past few days. \nHighs today and tomorrow will be in the low to mid 50s. Clearing\nskies and a relaxing pressure gradient tonight will allow lows \nto fall into the lower 30s inland and mid to upper 30s along the\ncoast. While not quite as breezy as yesterday, NNW winds will \nnevertheless occasionally gust to 15-20 mph this afternoon, \nbefore quickly diminishing after sunset.\n\nDry weather looks to prevail throughout the week ahead, as high\npressure builds into the area and becomes anchored overhead for\nthe mid to late week period. Aloft, the trough looks to finally\nmove offshore by late Monday into Tuesday, with upstream \nridging building in behind it. This pattern will support a \nmoderating temperature trend from midweek into next weekend. \nForecast highs reach the mid 60s by Wednesday, warming into the \nupper 60s to near 70 inland Thursday through Sunday. Even as the\nair mass turns milder, dry conditions and efficient radiational\ncooling under clear skies and light winds should still allow \nmorning lows to fall into the lower 30s across inland areas, \nwith upper 30s to low 40s closer to the coast. Unfortunately, there\nremains little to no prospect for welcome rainfall over the \nnext week to 10 days.",
